---
// The phase-0 about page (SPEC.md 7.3, 1.1, 1.7; D1). No island, no script: the "page with no tool"
// of SPEC.md 7.6 (15 kB). P1-A4 converts it to site/src/content/pages/about.mdx; the prose moves
// as it stands. No number with a golden value is written here (8.4).
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const title = 'Two Games on a Dynkin Diagram';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.endsWith('/') ? import.meta.env.BASE_URL : `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/`;
---

<Base title={`About: ${title}`}>
  <h1>About this site</h1>
  <p>
    {title} is the working title of this site. It teaches the mathematical intuition that connects two
    elementary games to Lie theory. You play N. J. Wildberger's mutation game and Mozes' numbers game on a
    Dynkin diagram, and watch the root system, the weights, the Weyl group, the root polytopes and the
    simplest representations grow out of your own moves. Later you decorate the same board with black nodes
    and arrows and meet the real forms through Satake diagrams. The games need only integer addition, so
    the entry cost is low; the destination is the structure theory that usually takes a graduate course to
    reach.
  </p>
  <p>
    The two views of a board are called Populations, for the mutation game, and Numbers, for the numbers
    game. The mutation game and its story of cities on Mars are Wildberger's. The numbers game is Mozes',
    with Proctor's earlier use of the same process and Eriksson's development of it acknowledged. The
    credits page gives the full credit.
  </p>
  <p>The site is being built in stages. This first stage has one board, on the home page.</p>
  <p><a href={base}>Play on the board</a> · <a href={`${base}credits/`}>Credits</a></p>
</Base>
